# Color pins, one "type,name,color" per line. Copy this file to
# ~/.config/say-hi/colors and edit it there - the copy in the tree is the default
# say-hi ships, and editing it dirties the checkout `hi --update` pulls into.
#
# type  = hosttag | username | hostname | usertag
# color = red, green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, brred, brgreen, bryellow, brblue, brmagenta, brcyan
# a hostname name holding * or ? is a pattern: "hostname,10.0.1.*,red" colors
# a whole subnet or domain at once. An exact pin and a hosttag both beat it.
# LOCALUSER and LOCALHOSTNAME are special username/hostname names that match
# the user/host of the machine say-hi is installed on, wherever that user/host
# shows up - e.g. "username,LOCALUSER,green" colors your own username green
# even after ssh'ing into a machine where you're logged in under that same
# name, without hardcoding the name itself. an exact "username,jeff,..." or
# "hostname,jeff-desktop,..." override still takes priority over these.
# anything with no pin at all falls back to a hash of the name.
# run `hi --color-preview` to preview what everything currently resolves to.
#type,name,color
hosttag,terminal,brmagenta
hosttag,baremetal,brred
hosttag,personal,brblue
hosttag,work,brgreen
hosttag,windows,bryellow
username,root,magenta
username,admin,red
username,ubuntu,yellow
username,debian,blue
usertag,personal,green
username,LOCALUSER,bryellow
hostname,LOCALHOSTNAME,brmagenta
